# Validation Report — CVE-2021-47124 (`io_uring: fix link timeout refs`) port to 5.10 target

- Target commit: `475312897ea6b4b9a9a56c838aec6370c42f7315`
- Reference fix: `ff4a96ba5c8f9b266706280ff8021d2ef3f17e86` (parent `a03e9fb7b8dd5d864020a658fc0ba9b6f264cd59`)
- Port mode / strategy: `history` / `history-trace`
- `validation_status`: **partial** (static + semantic pass; compile/runtime unavailable)

## 1. What the reference fix establishes

Upstream fix `a298232ee6b9` (`io_uring: fix link timeout refs`) states:

> `io_link_timeout_fn()` should put only one reference of the linked timeout
> request, however in case of racing with the master request's completion
> first `io_req_complete()` puts one and then `io_put_req_deferred()` is called.

Security invariant: **`io_link_timeout_fn()` must drop exactly one reference of the
linked timeout request (`data->req`) on every firing path**; the submission reference
was already dropped when the timer was armed, so the callback owns only the completion
reference. A second release causes a refcount underflow / premature free from the
hrtimer (softirq) context.

The supplied reference patch (`ff4a96ba`, 5.12/5.13 lineage) removes the redundant
unconditional trailing `io_put_req_deferred(req, 1)` after the `if/else` block:

```diff
 	if (prev) {
 		io_async_find_and_cancel(ctx, req, prev->user_data, -ETIME);
 		io_put_req_deferred(prev, 1);
 		io_put_req_deferred(req, 1);
 	} else {
 		io_req_complete_post(req, -ETIME, 0);
 	}
-	io_put_req_deferred(req, 1);
 	return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
```

## 2. Why the 5.10 target is affected

The target branch has a different structure (`req->link_list`, `io_cqring_add_event`),
so the reference hunk does not apply:

- `git apply --check` / `--recount` fail on context (exit 1).
- `git apply --3way` reports success but produces an **empty diff** — a no-op. Direct
  application therefore cannot deliver the fix.

The target code (`fs/io_uring.c`, `io_link_timeout_fn`, lines 6240–6275) is:

```c
	if (prev) {
		io_async_find_and_cancel(ctx, req, prev->user_data, -ETIME);
		io_put_req_deferred(prev, 1);
		io_put_req_deferred(req, 1);      /* line 6269 */
	} else {
		io_cqring_add_event(req, -ETIME, 0);
		io_put_req_deferred(req, 1);
	}
```

`io_async_find_and_cancel()` still ends with `io_put_req(req)` on the target branch
(line 5726), because the 5.10 branch never received the reference-side commit
`a03e9fb` that moved that put into `io_link_timeout_fn`. Therefore the `if (prev)` path
releases the linked timeout request **twice**.

History confirms the origin:

- Merge base of reference parent and target is `2c85ebc57b3e` (v5.10); the branches
  diverge after it (E-008).
- At the merge base the function was balanced — one put of `req` per path (E-012).
- `1c20e9040f49` (5.10 backport of the 5.12 introducing commit `9ae1f8dd372e0`)
  converted the else path to `io_cqring_add_event()` + `io_put_req_deferred(req, 1)`
  and kept one put per path (E-007).
- `0b2a990e5d2f` ("io_uring: fix link timeout refs", 5.10 backport of the *same*
  upstream commit as the reference) added `io_put_req_deferred(req, 1)` inside the
  `if (prev)` branch, assuming the helper no longer puts `req` — true only on the
  reference branch. On the target this introduced the extra release (E-006, E-011).
- `io_init_req()` sets `refs = 2` ("one is dropped after submission, the other at
  completion") and `io_queue_linked_timeout()` drops the submission reference when
  arming, so at timer fire `req->refs == 1` (E-013). The first put frees the request;
  the second put touches freed memory.

## 3. Candidate change

`generated.patch` (SHA-256 `0090f15355fbe620df5a88fa21cc0de856b84b0ddb4f7016776e7d79088deee2`):

```diff
diff --git a/fs/io_uring.c b/fs/io_uring.c
@@ -6266,7 +6266,6 @@ static enum hrtimer_restart io_link_timeout_fn(struct hrtimer *timer)
 	if (prev) {
 		io_async_find_and_cancel(ctx, req, prev->user_data, -ETIME);
 		io_put_req_deferred(prev, 1);
-		io_put_req_deferred(req, 1);
 	} else {
 		io_cqring_add_event(req, -ETIME, 0);
 		io_put_req_deferred(req, 1);
 	}
```

This is the exact analogue of the reference deletion: the redundant release of the
linked timeout request is removed, restoring one put per firing path. The helper
`io_async_find_and_cancel()` (shared with `IORING_OP_ASYNC_CANCEL`) and the `else`
branch are untouched.

## 4. Independent verification

Structural (from `validate_candidate.py`, `validation-evidence.json`):

- input checks all true; patch hash matches `application.json`;
- strict apply check / apply succeed on a clean target checkout;
- `git diff --check` passes, no conflict markers;
- planned paths `["fs/io_uring.c"]` == actual, no missing/unexpected.

Semantic review:

- independent re-read confirms `io_async_find_and_cancel()` still ends with
  `io_put_req(req)` and the candidate removes only the second release in the
  `if (prev)` branch;
- positive path: `if (prev)` → helper drops the completion ref once, `prev` released
  separately; `else` → single `io_put_req_deferred(req, 1)`;
- negative path: old code drove `refs` from 1 to 0 and then released again (UAF); the
  candidate removes that second release;
- compatibility: `io_put_req_deferred()` declaration/definition still present and
  used; `fs/Makefile:35` still builds `io_uring.o`; no new symbols/includes;
- `checkpatch.pl`: 0 errors, 0 warnings.

## 5. Not run

- Kernel/module compile: **not_run** — no `gcc`/`cc`/`clang`/`sparse`/`make` and no
  `.config` in the environment.
- Runtime reproduction of the linked-timeout refcount race: **not_run** — requires a
  built kernel and guest execution.

Because key build/runtime verification could not be performed for environmental
reasons, `validation_status` is `partial` (not `passed`). No semantic, coverage,
compatibility or structural finding failed.

## 6. Conclusion

The candidate correctly ports the CVE-2021-47124 security invariant to the 5.10 target:
`io_link_timeout_fn()` now releases the linked timeout request exactly once on each
firing path, removing the refcount underflow / use-after-free in the `if (prev)` path.
Structural and semantic validation pass; compile and runtime verification are recorded
as `not_run`.

